anubis (1.28.2-0+keel1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * New upstream release 1.28.2, from upstream's signed
    anubis-src-vendor-1.28.2.tar.gz (tag v1.28.2, signed). Security fixes
    since 1.27.0:
    - GHSA-5gwm-367w-7fgj (1.28.0): a client that controls the PTR
      records of its address could pass as a search engine crawler with
      several PTR records; reverse DNS is now forward-confirmed.
    - #1989 (1.28.0): the hardening of upstream's security review: path
      traversal in path rules, duplicate headers, query strings in
      forwarded URIs, JA4H set by the client, solutions bound to the
      issuing rule, DNSBL IPv6 answers and caching, no caching of
      challenge and error pages by CDNs, short HS512 secrets, malformed
      client addresses, honeypot log permissions, and more.
    - #1947 (1.28.0): redirect validation shared between pass-challenge
      and the subrequest mode; ambiguous redir forms are refused.
    - Go 1.27.3 or newer (CVE-2026-111943, upstream's changelog).
    Behaviour changes that reach Keel: a request with no or an empty
    User-Agent is denied ((data)/bots/spec-violations.yaml); the default
    policy denies domain fronting (X-Tls-Sni-Name other than Host); Thoth
    is gone (THOTH_* only warn); a missing verification cookie is retried
    once (#2004).
  * The WebAssembly proof-of-work modules that 1.28 needs at start come
    from upstream's signed anubis-src-vendor-npm-1.28.2.tar.gz, as the new
    gbp component wasm-modules/, checked against wasm-modules/SHA256SUMS;
    they cannot be built with trixie and trixie-backports today
    (debian/README.source). The wasm2js fallback is built here with
    upstream's binaryen 130 in the source, run by the vendored wazero.
    debian/copyright lists the licences of the crates in the modules
    (hashx and equix are LGPL-3.0-only, wee_alloc MPL-2.0).
  * npm component refreshed from the 1.28.2 package-lock.json: preact
    10.29.8, and wasm-feature-detect 1.9.0 (new).
  * Build with golang-1.27-go from trixie-backports.
  * autopkgtest: a challenge is told by its "randomData", as Anubis's
    error and deny pages carry the anubis_challenge script too.
  * Patches:
    - challenge-without-gzip.patch: rebased. 1.28.0 (#1958) answers the
      sampled refusal with the policy's DENY status, which is 200 in
      Keel's policy, so the test now checks the body as well (a
      challenge has "randomData"; every page has the anubis_challenge
      script). Upstream's TestNonGzipClientGetsDenyStatus, which asserts
      that the refusal happens, goes with the refusal.
    - redir-same-host.patch: rewritten for 1.28. Upstream's
      validateRedirect stays as it is; PassChallenge then also asks
      redirectAllowed, before both of its redirects (after a solved
      challenge, and the retry of a missing verification cookie). The
      subrequest mode is not changed. New test
      TestPassChallengeRedirectsToOwnOriginOnly.
    - redir-validate.patch: dropped, upstream's validateRedirect (#1947,
      1.28.0) has the same checks and tests /%2Fevil.com, /%2F%2Fevil.com
      and /%5Cevil.com.
    - keep-cookie-of-request-without-cookie.patch: dropped, upstream made
      the same fix (#1828, issue #1314, 1.28.0).
    - redirect-domains-ipv6.patch: dropped, Keel Web leaves
      REDIRECT_DOMAINS empty since 1.27.0-0+keel3 and nothing in Keel
      sets it. An IPv6 literal in REDIRECT_DOMAINS again stops anubis at
      start, as upstream does.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0000

anubis (1.27.0-0+keel4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Do not clear the auth cookie in the answer to a request that sent
    none (debian/patches/keep-cookie-of-request-without-cookie.patch).
    A subresource of the challenge page, sent before the pass-challenge
    answer set the cookie, was answered after it with a Set-Cookie that
    cleared the new cookie; the next page then passed, but its
    stylesheets and scripts got the challenge page. Seen on the WordPress
    replica of a cloud advanced pair: 8 of 147 shots of the login page
    had no style (BR2, 2026-11-07). An invalid or expired cookie is
    still cleared.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Sat, 07 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0000

anubis (1.27.0-0+keel3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Close pass-challenge's open redirect without REDIRECT_DOMAINS
    (debian/patches/redir-same-host.patch): with it empty, redir may only
    be relative or name the request's own scheme, host and port, never
    carry userinfo; upstream compared redir's host with itself. Every
    name a site is reached by keeps working, which a fixed
    REDIRECT_DOMAINS cannot promise.
  * Refuse redir forms a browser resolves to another host
    (debian/patches/redir-validate.patch, a backport of upstream's
    validateRedirect): backslashes (/\evil is //evil to a browser),
    control bytes, leading space, opaque URLs, a path starting with //,
    checked again after percent-decoding (/%2F/evil, /%5Cevil).
  * autopkgtest: pass-challenge behind nginx allows the same host by an
    unlisted name, a relative redir and an IPv6 literal, and refuses
    another host, //evil, userinfo and another IPv6 address, and /\evil,
    /%2F/evil, /%5Cevil, %09//evil and " //evil" as invalid.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 06:00:00 +0000

anubis (1.27.0-0+keel2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Serve the challenge to clients that do not accept gzip
    (debian/patches/challenge-without-gzip.patch). Upstream refused one
    such request in 64 at random with HTTP 500, and Keel Web answered 500
    to those clients (Keel-Linux/keel-web#2). A missing Accept-Encoding
    accepts any coding; the proof of work is the defence. The patch adds
    a test of 4096 requests without gzip, all challenged.
  * Accept IPv6 literals ([2001:db8::1]) in REDIRECT_DOMAINS
    (debian/patches/redirect-domains-ipv6.patch): Keel Web sets it to the
    machine's names and addresses to close pass-challenge's open
    redirect, and Anubis refused to start on an IPv6 entry.
  * debian/rules: TestChallengeFor_ErrNotFound, skipped as flaky because
    of that refusal, runs again.
  * autopkgtest: 512 requests without Accept-Encoding through nginx all
    get the challenge page with HTTP 200.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 04:00:00 +0000

anubis (1.27.0-0+keel1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Keel Linux build for trixie, based on the Debian Go team's
    work-in-progress packaging (salsa go-team/packages/anubis, ITP
    #1102132), which is kept as the debian/sid branch of this repository.
  * New upstream release 1.27.0, from upstream's signed
    anubis-src-vendor tarball (Go modules vendored) plus an npm component
    with the nine runtime JavaScript dependencies at the lockfile's
    versions; the build never uses the network.
  * Build with golang-1.26-go (trixie-backports), as go.mod requires
    1.26.3; build the challenge assets with trixie's esbuild, and xess
    with esbuild instead of postcss and cssnano.
  * Ship anubis@.service: a disabled template, DynamicUser, hardened; it
    refuses to start without /etc/anubis/<instance>.key, the signing key,
    which it copies into the runtime directory for the dynamic user.
  * Ship /etc/anubis/default.env (loopback only) and the upstream
    default policy as /etc/anubis/botPolicies.yaml.
  * Add an autopkgtest: the instance behind nginx challenges a browser
    and an unverified Googlebot, and refuses to start without a key.

 -- Marcos Méndez <mendez.foto@gmail.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

